### PASS B: AXIS0-ONLY STEERED ###

Sure! Let's imagine you have two magic dice that always show the same number when rolled, no matter how far apart they are.

### Step-by-Step Analogy:

#### 1. **The Dice Pair:**
   - You have these two special dice. No matter where you put them – in your pocket or on opposite sides of the world – if one shows a six, the other will also show a six.
  
#### 2. **Rolling the Dice:**
   - When you roll one die here at home, it randomly picks a number between 1 and 6 (like rolling any regular die). But guess what? The other die instantly knows which number was picked!
  
#### 3. **Separation Distance:**
   - Now, let’s say you take this die to your friend who lives across town. Even though there is now lots of space between you, whenever you roll your die, your friend’s die magically matches whatever number comes up!

### Why This Isn’t Special:
- In our normal experience with things like dice, we don't see something happening instantaneously over such large distances without anything connecting them physically. It feels like some kind of spooky action must be going on behind the scenes!

### Real Life Connection:
- Quantum entanglement works similarly but with tiny particles called "quantum particles" instead of dice. Scientists can separate these particles by huge distances, yet their properties remain connected just like those magical dice would stay linked even if separated.

### Conclusion:
- Just like your magic dice seem impossible because nothing could possibly connect them so quickly, scientists were surprised too when they discovered that certain pairs of very small particles behave exactly the same way—no matter how far apart they get from each other. That’s why people call it “spooky” or “weird,” but it turns out to be true according to experiments done today!

